timestamp=2026-11-05T12:51:55.868Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Let us also look at the full registry - all 24 farms in registry are the ones to return?\n# Task: \"fleet of 24 solar photovoltaic farms. Compute the total real energy production...\" and \"Return a dict keyed by `farm_id` with one entry per farm.\"\n# Also farm_registry.csv has 24 entries. So answer should have 24 keys. Some farms may have no valid readings -> 0.\n# The calibration info is only given for FARM_A to FARM_X in the JSON. For Y, Z, AA-AH that exist as production files but not in calib JSON - what about them?\n# Also look at what farm_ids are in registry - registry has A-X (24 total). Yes: A to X = 24 farms. So Y,Z,AA..AH production files might be for other farms not in the fleet? Or maybe they are extra data. Let us count registry again.\n# A-X gives 24 letters starting from A: A,B,C,...,X - that's 24 farms total (A is 1, X is 24th letter if starting from A as position 1 and counting A,B,C,...,X: A=0 index? A to J is 10, A to X: X is 23rd index if A is 0, so A-X is 24 total). Yes 24 farms. So registry = only these 24.\n# But production files also include Y,Z,AA-AH - maybe these are for farms in the registry under different names? Or maybe they are irrelevant? Let us look up Y,Z,AA-AH in farm_registry names? For example, FARM_Y, FARM_Z etc not in registry list.\n# Also look at farm names in registry: they go up to \"Savannah Array\" which is FARM_X. So Y,Z,AA-AH are probably not part of the 24-farm fleet. We need to compute totals for each farm - probably each farm in farm_registry, or each farm in farms.csv that corresponds to registry. Total fleet is 24 - so answer has 24 entries. Ignore production files for Y,Z,AA-AH? Or include if they map to something? Let us also check if their data has anything for the target day? But safer to focus on the 24 farms in registry (A-X) which also have calibration data in the JSON as given.\n# Also calibration JSON has only A-X (24 farms). So likely the 24 farms are A-X. The extra production files (Y onward) may be noise or for other purposes - or maybe part of the dataset. But let us look at maintenance notes - mentions specific farms by name, not IDs. Let us see all farm names in registry vs farms.csv - they map (e.g., Solar Park Alpha matches solar park alpha normalized). Yes. So the 24 farms are A-X. That means we should only process production files for FARM_A through FARM_X? Let us confirm by looking at the full task more.\n# \"An operator manages a fleet of 24 solar photovoltaic farms. Compute...\" Also 24 files in the A-X range (A to X is 24 farms). So Y,Z,AA-AH are probably irrelevant or possibly test data? Let us not include them. We should focus on A-X. That matches calibration data too.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
