timestamp=2026-11-05T12:53:49.323Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Units: KwH, kWh, Kwh - all kilowatt-hours (case-insensitive). MwH - megawatt-hours, 1 MWh = 1000 kWh. WH or wH - watt-hours, 1 kWh = 1000 Wh, so Wh/1000 = kWh. BTU/btu - British thermal unit; 1 kWh ≈ 3412.14 BTU (or sometimes 3.412e3). That’s a big conversion. Also kw/Kw/KW - kilowatts, which is power (instantaneous). But FARM_D,G,I etc are marked as reading_type 'power' in calib - and units kw. Also the task says \"compute total real energy production in kilowatt-hours (kWh)\". So for power (kW) readings, if they are instantaneous readings sampled over time (like every 30 min as we see), then integrating over time gives energy. e.g., kW × hours = kWh. Since timestamps are every 30 minutes (0:00, 0:30 etc), each interval is 0.5 hours. So for power readings, total energy = sum of (power_kW * 0.5h). We need to check reading_type.\n# Also look at calib for a power farm.\nimport json\nwith open('/app/dataset/sensors_calibration.json') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\nfarms = data['enterprise_platform']['deployment_environments']['system_config']['farms']\nfor fid, f in farms.items():\n    if f['sensor_info']['reading_type'] == 'power':\n        print(fid, f['sensor_info']['unit'], f['sensor_info']['reading_type'])\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
