timestamp=2026-11-05T12:53:56.913Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So 10 farms give power readings in kW. For these, to get energy over the interval, we need to integrate. If readings are at regular intervals (30 min), then sum(power * interval_hours). Usually for such datasets, each reading represents energy in that interval, or average power in interval. But with \"reading_type\": \"power\" and unit kW, and \"energy production\" total, most plausible is kW * 0.5h per 30-min reading. That’s standard. Let us verify with a sample of data - look at power values over time for a power-type farm.\n# Also look at FARM_D data\nimport pandas as pd\n\npath = '/app/dataset/production_FARM_D.csv'\nwith open(path, 'rb') as f:\n    b = f.read(2)\nenc = 'utf-16le' if b == b'\\xff\\xfe' else 'utf-8'\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(path, encoding=enc, sep=',', skiprows=3)\n# rename timestamp col\nif 'timestamp\\t' in df.columns:\n    df = df.rename(columns={'timestamp\\t': 'timestamp'})\n# show value column name from calib? FARM_D value col is power_reading  (with space)\ncols = [c for c in df.columns if 'power' in c]\nprint(cols)\nprint(df[cols].head(5))\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
