timestamp=2026-11-05T12:55:54.455Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So for FARM_D (power farm), the column specified by calib \"power_reading \" has values like 80.0 at night (baseline) and goes up during day. The other duplicate column gives different values (higher, already offset?). But calib offset is +80.0. That means (recorded - true) = 80.0, so true = recorded - 80.0. So for recorded 80.0 at night, true is 0.0. That matches expected (no production at night). Also for recorded ~254 at day, true is ~174. That fits. So the column named exactly as in calibration (with spaces preserved) is the recorded value. We must use the value column specified in calibration.\n# Also calibration says reading_type is power (kW) for FARM_D - so to get energy kWh, we need to multiply by time interval in hours. Timestamps are every 30 min (00:00, 00:30, 01:00...) so interval is 0.5h. So energy contribution per reading is power_kW * 0.5. Sum over all readings in the UTC window for 2024-03-15.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
timestamp=2026-11-05T12:55:54.455Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="tail -0" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
