timestamp=2026-11-05T12:59:22.120Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# FARM_C: header uses semicolon delimiter, data also semicolon. Also timestamps can be in different formats (14.03.2024 vs 2024-03-14) - need robust parsing. Also numbers use comma as decimal separator (35,1700) and sometimes thousands separator (18.000,0000) - e.g. 18.000,0000 means 18000.0 in some locales, but also notice calib offset for FARM_C is 18000.0 and value is in WH. That matches - reading column gives values like 18.000,0000? Let us look: in the line, last reading value before forecast_yield is \"18.000,0000\" for first data row, \"18.000,0000\" for second. That’s 18,000.0 (eighteen thousand) if dot is thousand sep and comma decimal. Also calibration offset is 18000.0. That means after applying offset (recorded - true = 18000.0), true would be recorded - 18000.0 = 0.0 at night? That fits. Also during day, recorded would be higher. So numbers use European format (dot as thousand separator, comma as decimal separator) in some cases.\n# Also FARM_AA had same format with commas as decimal. This is important for parsing values correctly.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
