timestamp=2026-11-05T13:37:04.409Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# FARM_T header has 'mwh_yield ' and 'mwh_yield' as separate columns (duplicate). In our dedup logic, we rename duplicates - but when we look for the value column specified in calib as 'mwh_yield ' (with space), after dedup the second occurrence becomes something else? Or we might be picking the wrong one.\n# Also look at their actual values: first 'mwh_yield ' (the one that might be original with space) gives 0.05 in first row? And second gives 0.0. Calibration offset for FARM_T is 0.05. So true = recorded - offset. If recorded is 0.05, true becomes 0.0 - matches. Also that suggests the column with value 0.05 is the recorded column matching calib's 'mwh_yield ' (with space). But after dedup, we need to make sure we pick the right one. Also in our dedup, we rename duplicates by adding suffix - but we look for the column name specified in calib. Let us see what happens after our dedup.\nDATASET = '/app/dataset'\nfid = 'FARM_T'\npath = f'{DATASET}/production_{fid}.csv'\nwith open(path, 'rb') as f:\n    b = f.read(2)\nenc = 'utf-16le' if b == b'\\xff\\xfe' else 'utf-8'\nsep = ','\nwith open(path, 'r', encoding=enc, errors='replace') as f:\n    for i, line in enumerate(f):\n        if i == 4:\n            sep = ';' if ';' in line else ','\n            break\ndf = pd.read_csv(path, encoding=enc, sep=sep, skiprows=3, engine='python')\ndf.columns = [str(c).strip().replace('\\t', '') for c in df.columns]\n# dedup like we did\ncols = pd.Series(df.columns)\nfor dup in cols[cols.duplicated()].unique():\n    cnt = 0\n    for i, c in enumerate(cols):\n        if c == dup:\n            cnt += 1\n            if cnt > 1:\n                cols.iloc[i] = f\"{dup}_{cnt}\"\ndf.columns = cols\nprint(list(df.columns))\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
