timestamp=2026-11-05T13:40:14.765Z level=INFO run=e7dc0897 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So value_true for FARM_T has values like 888888.75 and lots of 0s - very large numbers. Unit is MWh (energy). So each reading is ~888888 MWh of energy? That’s physically impossible, but that’s what the data says. Also offset is 0.05 - maybe the recorded value is around 888888.8, so true is ~888888.75. That’s huge.\n# But in our code for energy type, we sum the true values and convert units. For FARM_T, unit is MWh. To get kWh, we multiply by 1000. So 888888.75 MWh * 1000 = 888888750 kWh per reading if that’s the per-interval energy? That gives per-interval values in the hundreds of millions. Summing over many readings gives ~10^9 total - which matches our current result (~1.78e9). That matches. So the computation logic for FARM_T is producing the large number. Is this correct according to the data? Or could there be data quality issues like outliers (888888.8 appearing as a sentinel)?\n# Let us look at the raw values more - also in the task, we saw in FARM_A there were weird values like -999.0, 888888.8 in some contexts. Also safety protocols mention temperature > 85.0 causes invalidation for 45 min following spike - but no mention of other sentinel values. The other domain rules don’t mention outlier filtering except negative values (after offset), duplicates, maintenance windows, safety protocol, invalid timestamps. So maybe these large values are valid as given.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
